<h1>Text, theme &amp; locale</h1>
<p>This page covers HUD fonts, variables, theme styles, and localization. The text module can parse rich-text markup for scripts, but HUD nodes currently render plain text.</p>

<h2>Fonts</h2>
<p>A font is a <code>Font2D</code> asset. Reference it by URI on any text-bearing node:</p>
<pre><code>{
  "id": "title",
  "type": "label",
  "text": "Skyforge",
  "font": "asset://fonts/head",
  "font_size": 48
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>font</code> — an <code>asset://</code> URI naming a <code>Font2D</code> asset. Optional; the default is the embedded Inter Variable face.</li>
  <li><code>font_size</code> — height in canvas units. Scales with the canvas like every other dimension.</li>
</ul>
<p>Change them at runtime with <code>Hud.set_font(id, uri)</code> and <code>Hud.set_font_size(id, n)</code>; see <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'hud-from-lua'}) }}">HUD from Lua</a>.</p>

<h2>Wrapping, alignment, overflow</h2>
<p>Long text needs a policy: how it breaks, where it sits in its box, and what happens when it still does not fit.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "id": "objective",
  "type": "text",
  "text": "Hold the forge until the gate seals.",
  "text_wrap": "word",
  "text_alignment": "start",
  "text_vertical_alignment": "center",
  "line_spacing": 4,
  "max_lines": 3,
  "text_overflow": "ellipsis",
  "size": [420, 96]
}</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Values</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>text_wrap</code></td><td><code>none</code>, <code>word</code>, <code>grapheme</code></td><td>Never break; break at word boundaries; break between any two characters (for narrow CJK columns).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>text_alignment</code></td><td><code>start</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>end</code></td><td>Horizontal placement of each line in the box.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>text_vertical_alignment</code></td><td><code>start</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>end</code></td><td>Where the text block sits vertically in the box.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>line_spacing</code></td><td>number</td><td>Extra canvas units between lines.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>max_lines</code></td><td>number</td><td>Caps how many lines are drawn.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>text_overflow</code></td><td><code>visible</code>, <code>clip</code>, <code>ellipsis</code></td><td>Draw past the box; cut at the box edge; cut with “…”.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The example renders up to three wrapped lines, centered vertically, left-aligned, and elides with “…” if the objective text still overflows.</p>

<h2>${variables} interpolation</h2>
<p>Text can reference named variables. At draw time each <code>${name}</code> is replaced with the variable’s current value.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "variables": ["score"],
  "children": [
    { "id": "score", "type": "label", "text": "Score: ${score}" }
  ]
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li>Top-level <code>variables</code> in the HUD document is an array of variable <em>names</em> — never a name→value map. Values are runtime strings set from Lua with <code>Hud.set_variable</code> / <code>Hud.set_variables</code>; unresolved names stay visible as <code>${name}</code>.</li>
  <li>Node <code>text</code> may mix literal words and <code>${name}</code> markers anywhere in the string.</li>
  <li>Interpolation happens when text is displayed, so one node definition can show changing values.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>local Hud = require("demi.hud")

Hud.set_variable("score", "1200")
Hud.set_variables({ score = "1300", game_title = "Skyforge II" })
local has = Hud.has_variable("score")      -- boolean
local v = Hud.get_variable("score")        -- string or nil
Hud.reset_variables()                      -- clear runtime values</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Hud.set_variable(name, value)</code> sets one value (a string). Returns <code>true</code> when something changed, plus an error string.</li>
  <li><code>Hud.set_variables(table)</code> sets several at once. Same return pair.</li>
  <li><code>Hud.has_variable(name)</code> returns a boolean.</li>
  <li><code>Hud.get_variable(name)</code> returns the current string, or <code>nil</code> when the name is unknown.</li>
  <li><code>Hud.reset_variables()</code> clears every runtime value; unresolved names render as <code>${name}</code>. No return value.</li>
</ul>
<p>The score label redraws with the new number — no need to rebuild the node.</p>

<h2>Rich text tags</h2>
<p><code>Text.parse_rich(markup, strict?)</code> parses supported tags into plain text, spans, and diagnostics for scripts. HUD labels do not consume those spans, so putting tags directly in a node's <code>text</code> does not style it.</p>
<pre><code>local Text = require("demi.text")
local parsed = Text.parse_rich("[strong]Forge tip:[/strong] hold guard")
-- parsed.text, parsed.spans, parsed.diagnostics</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Tag</th><th>Parsed metadata</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>[color=…][/color]</code></td><td>A color span with a value such as <code>#ffd27a</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>[em][/em]</code></td><td>An emphasis span.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>[strong][/strong]</code></td><td>A strong span.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>[link=…][/link]</code></td><td>A link span with a value.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>[icon=…]</code></td><td>A zero-length icon span with a value.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>The parser only describes spans; it does not make HUD text bold, italic, linked, or insert an image.</p>

<h2>Theme styles</h2>
<p>A <strong>theme</strong> is a JSON document of reusable styles. Reference one from the HUD and apply it per node with <code>style</code>.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "styles": {
    "menu_button": {
      "font_size": 28,
      "color": "#f2f4ff",
      "background_color": "#2a2f45ff",
      "padding": [24, 12, 24, 12],
      "min_size": [280, 52]
    },
    "title": {
      "font_size": 48,
      "color": "#ffffff"
    }
  },
  "action_map": { "submit": "ui_ok" }
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>styles</code> — map of style name to supported fields: <code>color</code>, <code>background_color</code>, <code>text_color</code>, <code>padding</code>, <code>gap</code>, <code>font_size</code>, <code>min_size</code>, <code>control_height</code>, and <code>row_height</code>. Set <code>font</code> directly on the node.</li>
  <li><code>action_map</code> — optional map from HUD navigation slots (<code>next</code>, <code>previous</code>, <code>submit</code>, <code>cancel</code>) to project input action names.</li>
</ul>
<p>Applying a style in a HUD document:</p>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "theme": "../ui/themes/dark.json",
  "children": [
    { "id": "title", "type": "label", "text": "${game_title}", "style": "title" },
    { "id": "start", "type": "button", "text": "Start", "style": "menu_button", "action": "ui_ok" }
  ]
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li>Top-level <code>theme</code> names the theme file path, relative to the HUD file.</li>
  <li><code>style</code> on a node copies that style’s fields onto the node.</li>
  <li>Explicit node <code>text_color</code>, <code>font_size</code>, and <code>min_size</code> take precedence. Styles can still replace node <code>color</code> and <code>background_color</code>; they fill padding and gap when those values are zero.</li>
</ul>
<p>Why themes: one change to <code>menu_button</code> restyles every menu button in Skyforge. Individual exceptions stay readable on the node.</p>

<h2>Localization</h2>
<p>A <strong>localization file</strong> supplies initially loaded strings. The HUD references one file; each node pulls its string by key when the document loads. Runtime locale switching uses the HUD's embedded <code>locales</code> map.</p>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "localization": {
    "play": "Spielen",
    "quit": "Beenden"
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "localization_file": "../ui/de.localization.json",
  "children": [
    { "id": "start", "type": "button", "text": "Start", "localization_key": "play", "style": "menu_button" },
    { "id": "seed", "type": "text_input", "placeholder_localization_key": "seed_hint" }
  ]
}</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li>The file holds a flat <code>localization</code> object mapping string keys to translated text. A HUD loads only the file named by <code>localization_file</code>.</li>
  <li><code>localization_file</code> on the HUD names that file, relative to the HUD file.</li>
  <li>A node’s <code>localization_key</code> (and <code>placeholder_localization_key</code> for text inputs) selects the string; its value replaces the node’s text (or placeholder) when the document loads.</li>
  <li>For runtime switching, embed a <code>locales</code> object plus a default <code>locale</code> in the HUD:</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>{
  "format_version": 1,
  "locale": "en",
  "locales": {
    "en": { "play": "Start", "quit": "Quit" },
    "de": { "play": "Spielen", "quit": "Beenden" }
  },
  "children": [
    { "id": "start", "type": "button", "text": "Start", "localization_key": "play" }
  ]
}</code></pre>
<pre><code>local Hud = require("demi.hud")

Hud.set_locale("de")             -- reapply every localization key in German</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Hud.set_locale(code)</code> selects a loaded entry in <code>locales</code> and reapplies localization keys. It cannot load another <code>localization_file</code>. Returns <code>true</code> on success, plus an error string; the last valid locale is kept on failure.</li>
  <li><code>${name}</code> variables are a separate native-variable mechanism — they are interpolated, not localized.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Pseudo-locale QA</h2>
<p>Translated text is often longer than English. <strong>Pseudo-localization</strong> brackets strings and repeats vowels so some layout bugs show up before real translations arrive.</p>
<pre><code>Hud.set_pseudo_locale(true)      -- stress-test layout with fake translations</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Hud.set_pseudo_locale(enabled)</code> takes a boolean and switches displayed strings to a pseudo-locale build of the current strings. No return value.</li>
  <li>Localized strings render with doubled vowels inside brackets (English “Start” becomes “[Staart]”), making some truncation and wrapping problems easier to see.</li>
  <li>Run Skyforge’s menu in pseudo-locale before each release; fix anything that clips or overflows.</li>
</ul>

